+<section id="inventory" class="section"><header><span>Items, bags, and rooms</span><h2>Inventory</h2></header><p>Inventory is a JSON-serializable hierarchy of named items and stable, named bag instances. Item stacks hold up to 1000 units. Compatible ordinary bags also stack up to 1000 while empty; adding an item or child bag automatically splits one bag from the stack so every populated bag has its own identity.</p><div class="columns"><div><h3>Nested bags</h3><ul><li>Populated bags may contain item stacks and other populated bags to any valid depth.</li><li>Moving a bag moves its complete nested branch without flattening or redistributing it.</li><li>A single click opens a bag and shows its immediate contents.</li><li>A double-click unpacks its immediate contents into the parent.</li><li>The visible <b>Take Everything</b> action is the accessible alternative to double-clicking.</li></ul></div><div><h3>Rooms are locations</h3><ul><li>A <b>safe room</b> preserves its complete nested hierarchy when the player leaves.</li><li>A <b>danger room</b> recursively clears all of its contents on exit.</li><li>Room bags are fixed, top-level locations. Unlike movable ordinary bags, they cannot be put inside, moved into, or unpacked into another bag.</li></ul></div></div><div class="note"><b>Stacks and splitting.</b> Define each item by name, then add quantities from 1 through 1000. Empty bags stack only when their definition, name, and properties are compatible. A populated bag is always a single instance.</div><h3>Define and build an inventory</h3><div class="code"><div class="codebar"><span>Story JavaScript</span><button type="button">Copy</button></div><pre>Inventory.defineItem("vial", { name: "Healing vial" });
+
+const pack = Inventory.createBag("travel-pack", {
+  name: "Travel pack",
+  properties: { capacity: 12 }
+});
+const pouch = Inventory.createBag("medicine-pouch", {
+  name: "Medicine pouch",
+  parentId: pack.id,
+  quantity: 3
+});
+const camp = Inventory.createRoom("camp", {
+  name: "Camp",
+  safe: true
+});
+
+Inventory.addItem(pouch.id, "vial", 6);
+Inventory.moveBag(pouch.id, pack.id);</pre></div><h3>Open, unpack, and enter rooms</h3><div class="code"><div class="codebar"><span>Story JavaScript</span><button type="button">Copy</button></div><pre>Inventory.openBag(pack.id);              // Show this bag directly.
+Inventory.unpackBag(pouch.id, pack.id);  // Transfer immediate contents.
+
+Inventory.enterRoom(camp.id);
+Inventory.leaveRoom(camp.id);            // Safe room hierarchy remains.
+
+Inventory.open();                        // Open the main Inventory browser.</pre></div><div class="table"><div><code>Inventory.defineItem(id, definition)</code><span>Register a named item definition.</span></div><div><code>Inventory.createBag(definition, options?)</code><span>Create or stack compatible empty ordinary bags.</span></div><div><code>Inventory.createRoom(definition, options?)</code><span>Create a fixed top-level safe or danger room bag.</span></div><div><code>Inventory.addItem(bagId, item, quantity?)</code><span>Add up to 1000 units, splitting a bag stack when needed.</span></div><div><code>Inventory.moveBag(sourceId, destinationId)</code><span>Move an ordinary bag and its complete nested branch.</span></div><div><code>Inventory.openBag(id)</code><span>Show one bag and its immediate rows.</span></div><div><code>Inventory.unpackBag(id, destinationId)</code><span>Move only the immediate items and child bags.</span></div><div><code>Inventory.enterRoom(id)</code><span>Enter a top-level room location.</span></div><div><code>Inventory.leaveRoom(id)</code><span>Leave it, preserving safe rooms or clearing danger rooms recursively.</span></div><div><code>Inventory.open()</code><span>Open the built-in Inventory browser.</span></div></div><div class="warning"><b>Hierarchy changes are guarded and atomic.</b><p>A bag cannot become its own ancestor or be moved or unpacked into one of its descendants. Duplex validates ancestry, room, compatibility, and capacity restrictions before changing anything. If unpacking would fail any check, the entire operation fails and the source and destination remain unchanged.</p></div></section>
+
+<section id="saves" class="section"><header><span>Player safety</span><h2>Saves and portable backups</h2></header><p>Duplex keeps one browser save per story. Players can export that progress as a JSON file and import it in another browser or device. Imported files are checked for a Duplex marker, supported schema, matching story identity, valid history, and known passages before progress is replaced. The complete JSON-serializable inventory hierarchy participates in <b>Save</b>, import/export, <b>Back</b>, <b>Restart</b>, and history restoration, including every nested item, ordinary bag, and room tree.</p><div class="columns"><div><h3>Save dialog</h3><ul><li><b>Save</b> stores progress in this browser.</li><li><b>Load</b> restores the browser save.</li><li><b>Export</b> downloads a portable JSON backup.</li><li><b>Import</b> validates and restores a backup.</li><li><b>Delete</b> removes the browser save.</li></ul></div><div><h3>JavaScript API</h3><div class="table"><div><code>Save.save()</code><span>Store the browser save.</span></div><div><code>Save.load()</code><span>Restore it.</span></div><div><code>Save.delete()</code><span>Remove it.</span></div><div><code>Save.export(filename?)</code><span>Download JSON.</span></div><div><code>Save.import(source)</code><span>Import JSON, an object, or a File.</span></div><div><code>Save.serialize()</code><span>Return JSON text.</span></div><div><code>Save.parse(text)</code><span>Validate JSON text.</span></div></div></div></div><div class="code"><div class="codebar"><span>Story JavaScript</span><button type="button">Copy</button></div><pre>Save.save();
 Save.export();
 
 // File imports return a promise.
 Save.import(file).then(() =&gt; {
   UI.alert("Save imported.");
 });</pre></div><p class="note"><code>Save.import(file)</code> is asynchronous when given a browser <code>File</code>. The API is available as both <code>Save</code> and <code>Duplex.Save</code>. Duplex still has one browser slot rather than SugarCube’s full multi-slot system.</p></section>
 
